\subsection{Front matter}{

\code{front_matter} holds what comes before the body, e.g. a title page, a signature
table and a table of contents (\code{\link[=pub_toc]{pub_toc()}}). It takes the same outputs as the
children (also lists of them) and replaces the title block (in HTML the header), in
the viewer, in \code{\link[=pub_html]{pub_html()}} and in every format of \code{\link[=pub_render]{pub_render()}}:
\itemize{
\item Its sections are unnumbered (the numbers of the body start at 1), while its tables
and figures count in the caption numbers.
\item The table of contents of a report spec comes after it; end it with
\code{\link[=pub_pagebreak]{pub_pagebreak()}} to start the contents on a new page.
\item \code{list()} drops the title block without putting anything in its place.
\item The \code{front_matter} function of a \code{\link[=pub_report_spec]{pub_report_spec()}} replaces it (and can build
on it: \code{doc$front_matter}), e.g. for a title block of one format only.
}
}

\subsection{Failed steps}{

A failed step does not have to stop the document: write the inputs of a layout step
as \code{~try(step)} and run the pipeline with
\code{pipeflow::pip_run(x, on_error = "continue")}. The document can then still be
rendered and shows an error box with the step's error message where the step's
output would be (also for steps that did not run). In contrast, with a plain \code{~step}
the layout step is skipped if the step fails, so there is no document. With the
default \code{pipeflow::pip_run(x, on_error = "stop")} the run stops immediately at the
first failure.
}

\subsection{Saving documents}{

A document holds only the outputs, not the pipeline, so it can be saved with
\code{\link[=saveRDS]{saveRDS()}} and rendered later or in another R session. One pitfall: a ggplot
remembers where it was made. If a plot step is written as a lambda inside the
function that builds the pipeline (\verb{\\(data = ~data) ggplot(...)}), the saved
document includes the whole pipeline. To avoid this, define plot functions outside
the function that builds the pipeline. Plots that a step renders itself
(\code{pub_figure(render = TRUE)}) have no such problem.
}
}
\examples{
p <- pipeflow::pip_new("demo") |>
    # Steps that make the content
    pipeflow::pip_add("data", \(n = 4) {
        data.frame(x = seq_len(n), y = seq_len(n)^2)
    }) |>
    pipeflow::pip_add("table", \(data = ~data) {
        pub_table(data, caption = "Data")
    }) |>
    pipeflow::pip_add("mean", \(data = ~data) {
        pub_format_num(mean(data$y), "Mean: \%.2f")
    }) |>
    # The layout step arranges their outputs into a document
    pipeflow::pip_add("report", \(table = ~table, mean = ~mean) {
        pub_doc(
            pub_section("Data", table),
            pub_section("Results", mean),
            title = "Demo"
        )
    })

# Run the pipeline and take the document from the layout step
pipeflow::pip_run(p, lgr = NULL)
doc <- p[["report", "out"]]
doc

# A document can also be made directly, without a pipeline
pub_doc(
    pub_section(
        "Introduction",
        pub_text("The data cover **153** days, from May to September."),
        as.data.frame(table(Month = airquality$Month), responseName = "Days")
    ),
    pub_section("Annex", pub_session_info(), numbered = FALSE),
    title = "Analysis report",
    meta = list(author = "Jane Doe", app = "demo", version = "1.0")
)
}
